{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"EPIC CLOSE ASSESSMENT — challenged on whether this can close with 5 open issues. Checking the ACs\nrather than arguing the principle produced a MORE serious answer than the question implied.\n\nTHE HEADLINE AC IS NOT MET, AND I WAS ABOUT TO CLOSE ON A MISREAD.\n\n  '[operator-attested] ... at least one issue created in DC appears in the local store after an\n   inbound pass, and one local edit appears on the DC issue after an outbound pass, with zero\n   unhandled exceptions'\n\nWhat the live run (30637886718) actually proved:\n    invariants: scanned=0 filed=0\n    OK: steady-state pass converged\n    RECON: outbound_differ total=0\n    RECON: inbound_differ total=0\n\nSCANNED ZERO. Nothing was synced because nothing existed to sync. That demonstrates the pass runs\ncleanly against an EMPTY instance — not that bidirectional reconciliation MOVES DATA. The only test\nthat would show data moving is the provenance test (creates a DC issue, looks for it locally), and it\nfailed on my own envelope bug and has NOT been re-run since I fixed that (change 1106, gates pending).\n\nSo I would have closed this epic on 'no errors' with zero evidence of function — the exact failure\nmode this epic has now produced five separate times. 'Converged' is not 'works'; on an empty store\nthey are indistinguishable, and only one of them is the acceptance criterion.\n\nON THE FIVE OPEN TICKETS — they do not divide evenly, and the framing understates one and overstates\nthree:\n\n BLOCKING, in my judgement:\n * [rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] DC index lag makes recover_pending unbind a live create -> DUPLICATE\n   Jira issues. DC-specific, corrupts data, and lives in the exact code path this epic delivers.\n   Closing an epic titled 'Jira Data Center support' while a DC-specific race writes duplicates into a\n   customer's Jira is not a defensible boundary.\n\n SHOULD FIX, because it degrades the ORACLE this epic's headline AC depends on:\n * [rebar:f391-806b-938f-45d8] the harness exhausts Jira DC's 10-PAT cap. Test infrastructure, but it\n   has now recurred in three consecutive runs and J9 added more consumers. An epic whose acceptance is\n   operator-attested against a harness cannot tolerate a harness that fails at setup.\n\n DEFENSIBLE FOLLOW-UPS — pre-existing defects this epic SURFACED rather than introduced, neither\n DC-specific:\n * [rebar:387d-09c5-3d50-4a1e] a failed identity write deletes the created issue (affects Cloud too).\n * [rebar:7c26-4ac8-04a3-440e] bindings key on the Jira KEY (Cloud has always had this).\n\n GENUINE SCOPE CALL:\n * [rebar:b586-2fda-8a15-4260] DC rate limiting. DC-only, but no AC requires it, it is OFF BY DEFAULT,\n   and the fix is header-reactive rather than a feature.\n\nWHAT I AM DOING: not closing. The next step is to land 1106, re-run the live harness, and get REAL\nbidirectional evidence — an issue created in DC appearing locally, and a local edit appearing in DC.\nIf that evidence does not materialise, the epic stays open regardless of how many stories are closed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785509411631822001,"uuid":"494d9f9d-d12c-4fdb-a476-17e29e2672d8"}